## Core Findings & Strategic Tension for P4

1. **Challenging the "Rare Biosphere" Defense for Thermoplasmatota:**  
   [Maeke et al. (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1186/s40168-025-02140-8) examined the novel Thermoplasmatota class *Ca.* Penumbrarchaeia and concluded that its record-high fraction of unknown genes is driven by environmental rarity ("rare biosphere"). P4 directly refutes this: the Rub' al-Khali Thermoplasmatota are cosmopolitan (detected in 481 of 549 samples), and P4's 1:1 nearest-neighbor matched null proves they remain **4.5- to 6.5-fold darker than bacteria and other archaea** matched on abundance, prevalence, completeness, and size.
2. **Archaeal Genetic Codes & Gene-Calling Truncation Artifacts:**  
   Recent discoveries—including **Code 34** ([Kivenson et al. 2025, *Science*](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adu2404)), dynamic amber UAG ambiguity ([Shalvarjian et al. 2025, *PNAS*](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2517473122)), and high-throughput k-mer detection ([Melnykov 2026, *PNAS*](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2610659123))—show that uncultivated Archaea frequently reassign stop codons. Because P4 ran Pyrodigal in meta mode with **Table 11**, any internal TAG or recoded stop codon prematurely truncates ORFs ([Borges et al. 2022, *Nature*](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-022-04715-x); [Ivanova et al. 2014, *Science*](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1250691)). This provides a compelling alternative hypothesis for why 76% of P4's dark proteins are $<100$ aa, necessitating an explicit Methods caveat.
3. **Reference Database Ceilings:**  
   [Albright & Louca (2023)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-01994-7) demonstrates that reference databases (RefSeq, KEGG) systematically underrepresent uncultivated taxa and Archaea ($<3\%$). This validates P4's 55.6% no-KO ceiling as a database constraint rather than an assembly artifact.
4. **MAG vs. Cultivated Accessory Decoupling & Completeness Confounding:**  
   In 23 shared bacterial species, P4 found isolates and MAGs share 80% annotated but only 16% dark proteins. Critical pangenomic literature ([Pellegrinetti et al. 2026, *Front Microbiol*](https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1884628); [Hellewell et al. 2024, *Bioinformatics*](https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btae542); [Majernik et al. 2025, *mSphere*](https://doi.org/10.1128/msphere.00857-24); [Tang & Yin 2023, *Brief Bioinform*](https://doi.org/10.1093/bib/bbac484)) shows that assembly fragmentation at contig boundaries and incompleteness selectively drop core genes into the accessory pool and purge repetitive accessory islands, inflating apparent accessory divergence. Furthermore, all 23 cultivated pairs are Bacteria; none are Archaea.

